[Applause]  thank you so much Rebecca for the  introduction and also for some of the  other conveners and especially  authorship on how Mirage for inviting me  to moderate this panel I just have a  quick question is there some light up  here that we can use okay I got it it's  great okay so let me open this panel  with a few thoughts on the key theme of  our symposium being material and that  which is livable the theme of our panel  today as I think through these themes  also through my own work so what does it  mean to be material today at a time when  the limits of extracting the earth's  resources for profit are increasingly  becoming visible across the globe with  toxic leakages rising global  temperatures and the loss of hospitable  environments for both humans and  nonhumans many cultural critics  scientists and artists have recently  turned their attention towards  materiality and ecological processes the  hope of this turn toward the material  art argue is to reflect on their earthly  and often unintended consequences of  human structures of design as well as  the material conditions of social  inequality as they were produced in the  context of war resource resource  extraction and displacement to think  about scales in crucial here if we want  to account for the intertwined  relationships between the material and  the social the organic and artificial  we've talked about some of that  yesterday in some of the panels and  that's also to get a better sense of the  entanglement between bodies with a  microbes minerals plants or human bodies  on one hand and technologies on the  other hand so in my own work I've  approached being materials from a  particular place the city of Berlin  Berlin is a city that is so heavily  saturated with narratives about the West  and the violence that has  Cape 20th and 21st your century European  history of fascism displacement the Cold  War and the collapse of socialism all of  which can be traced in the cities  material fabric for this very reason the  city provides for me a productive site  from which to figure out how to create  alternative stories about material  things to illustrate that let me start  off with the curious story of a plant  that has occurred at a moment in which  the city's material fabric literally  burst apart and gave way to a sense of  the city as more than human habitat and  what I call ruderal City view of the  city in which nature is not there to be  managed controlled incorporated in  forensic organic urban landscapes we had  infrastructure and technology but an  integral and unwieldy part of the city  toward the end of World War two large  rubble fields started the heavily bombed  city of Berlin as the city grew  increasingly divided with the onset of  the Cold War many non-native plants  flourished in the city's rubble for the  first time one of these plants was Sikhi  goosefoot okay no podium buttress a  yellow-green flowering plant covered  with sticky hairs and a strong aromatic  scent sticky goosefoot was originally  imported from constantinople and  cultivated in gardens throughout europe  at the beginning of the sixteenth  century as a plant prospered for the  first time more widely beyond cultivated  gardens in Berlin's blasted landscapes  their widespread emergence triggered the  curiosity of West Berlin botanist  surrounded by the Berlin Wall and cut  off from their usual field sites in the  countryside to study the so-called  ruderal ecology of the city one of the  botanists who often now is referred to  as the father of urban ecology was  Harrison cook and here was an image of  him in the young verbena forest  so now the word ruderal comes from  Rudess which is the Latin word for  rubble it's a common term in urban  ecology that is used for plants such as  Keenan podium buttress they've produced  large numbers of seeds and are usually  the first to colonize disturbed  environments such as the sides of roads  and train tracks urban wastelands a  rubble field now Rudo plants have been  good for me to think to think west and  specifically approaching the question of  material materiality and life in my case  urban life on an increasingly damaged  planet rudos get us to think about three  aspects that I think will also hear more  about from our speakers today soil value  scale so in terms of soil rude rules  like C key goosefoot are able to grow an  environment in the city that are usually  considered hostile to life such as the  spaces alongside the city's  infrastructure or in rubble they depend  on an edge effect of contrasting  environments and they prefer dry  gravelly soils that are poor in  nutrients in fact they grow in soil that  is grow and thrive in soil that is often  at least partially covered with a  foreign substance such as cement or they  grow in their cracks as you can see in  this image value the first to inhabit  disturbed land disturbed lands rules are  not a nuisance in the sense of weeds  although they may be considered to be by  some they set the stage for other  flourishing ecosystems for example by  giving rise to spontaneous wetlands  attracting birds or providing shade and  organic matter important for other  species yet despite these ecological  benefits after world war two rules were  not simply a sign of regenerating nature  or a new life emerging out of the ruins  of the war as is depicted on the fifty  senac 50 pfennig coin that you see here  in the image camara commemorating not  only the rubble women but also women  reforesting the lock down forest and  thus rebuilding nature after the war  so rude rules instead where instead  where the outcome of a much more  ambivalent and in fact sticky and  radically changing urban ecology amid  the blasts blasted landscapes of war and  ecological disturbance by the mid 20th  century Berlin like other cities has  become hotter due to increasing traffic  densely built environments and  additional heat sources in the city and  on earth Saru Durrell's were that's not  only indicators with the city coming  back to life as was so key to the kind  of national narrative after the war but  they were also warning sign of global  environmental change and the destruction  that nationalism and racism have wrought  across the world after World War Two and  continues to do so today what is  interesting here is also that there is  an elemental to unexpected and  unrealized cosmopolitan potentials  hidden in the city the seeds of rural  plants have found their way into the  city via divers transport routes some of  them had crossed the city on the boots  of refugees or soldiers during the war  or ice via packaging material from  imported goods or via hay transported on  wagons by the Russian army other seeds  routes remained unknown now in my own is  no graphic work the story of ruderal  plants has led me to think across  different scales of analysis and engage  unlikely human and non-human neighbors  that shape urban life in Berlin so some  of these are the rubble of war but also  plants growing in its ruins rabbits  inhabiting the city's border  infrastructures as senior in an image in  the film rabbit alla Berlin to makeshift  urban gardens built by Turkish  immigrants in the great zones of the  nation staged here we see an image of a  Turkish garden that was built in the  shadow of the Berlin Wall and actually  built around a rule  three decree of him heaven - rusty  fences around refugee homes set up on  former military bases in the East German  forests where people who flee war and  conflict in this case Syria but also  Kenya and other places across Africa  once again inhabit Europe's ruins while  they try to get a new life on track now  as we'll think more through the  questions of the material in living and  dying in the following presentations  across different scale such as bacteria  oil money and other material matters we  don't only encounter life and livelihood  but also histories of violence  Deacon's decomposition and death now  feminists have had a lot to say about  how we relate to matter we are our tools  yet too often the history of technology  has been the history of man's  externalization in contrast animals  plants and matter but also women and  racialized others have been seen as  stuck in the in processes of  internalization simply adapting to  technology or controlled by it  this vitalism of technology depends on a  gendered and racialized binary between  technology as a form of externalization  and active and matter on the other hand  as passive or degenerate a feminist  science fiction writer whose alala grin  points out in her essay the carrier  bacteria fiction once you remove the  knife and spares from the human hero in  the history of tool-making and redefine  the emergence of culture not as one of  domination or exploitation but what but  as one of carrier back collection the  hero begins to look a bit more mundane  in fact without the Podesta of heroic  tool-making put in a pouch or basket he  looks more like a rabbit or a potato  perhaps in our analysis of the livable  end of being material we need a bit less  pipes networks and wires and instead  collect more stories about rabid  basket speeds and other illegal  hitchhikers and what about roadkill as  having said that and also getting ready  for the March for Sciences afternoon I'd  like to open our panel with the question  what might it mean to reclaim a  different sense of the material today  what might it mean to do work that both  focuses on damage but also on the desire  to make things livable and with that I  hand over the stage to Tom  [Applause]

